@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+# Daily Grind
+
+`npm start` serves the homepage and its local assets with Node (port `11222`, or `PORT`). There are no runtime dependencies or build steps. Use Node 20 or newer. In production, terminate HTTPS at a reverse proxy that preserves the original Host header and passes response security headers through unchanged.
+
+Newsletter submissions POST to `/api/newsletter`. Valid email addresses and signup timestamps are appended to `data/newsletter.jsonl`, or the path in `NEWSLETTER_FILE`. Put this path on persistent, private storage in production. The server never exposes this directory. A signup succeeds only after the write completes; sending newsletter campaigns is outside this endpoint's scope. The native HTML form also works without JavaScript.
+
+Every response includes an enforced `Connection-Allowlist: (response-origin "https://images.unsplash.com")`. Redirects and WebRTC retain their blocked defaults. CSP additionally permits same-origin form submissions and fetches, the Unsplash image host for photos, local styles, and only the nonce-authorized signup script. Workers, frames, objects, inline handlers, and base URL overrides are blocked. No outbound reporting service is used.
+
+Connection-Allowlist support varies by browser and cannot be polyfilled. CSP provides fetch, subresource, and form restrictions in other browsers, but does not cover all network-layer channels (such as WebRTC or speculative connections). Full network-layer enforcement requires a browser supporting Connection-Allowlist. The allowed Unsplash host remains a trusted destination.
+
+`npm test` checks headers, validation, local persistence, native form fallback, and customer-facing failure states using mocked HTTP streams and a JavaScript VM. It opens no listening sockets and does not import the server entrypoint.
